    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nRepro:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal ns = vim.api.nvim_create_namespace(\"repro\")\r\nvim.ui_attach(ns, { ext_messages = true }, function(event, ...)\r\n  if event == \"msg_show\" then\r\n    vim.cmd(\"highlight Normal\")\r\n  end\r\nend)\r\n\r\nprint(\"\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nReduced down from a more complex setup where the trigger was `:=some lua code` as a shorthand.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nAdd a missing guard in `msg_puts_len`, similar to the one in `msg_ext_ui_flush`\r\n\r\n## Trace\r\n\r\n`message.c` prevents other messages being emitted while it's emitting a chunk:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/521eddec7c243a8166609bf1734cb940e9510fc5/src/nvim/message.c#L389\r\n\r\nThis is asserted during the chunk output:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/521eddec7c243a8166609bf1734cb940e9510fc5/src/nvim/message.c#L400\r\n\r\nThe assertion fails if:\r\n- A ui callback is attached (`ui_attach()`) with message side-effects\r\n- An [empty string is emitted] (this sets `msg_ext_kind` to `\"lua_print\"` and then triggers a `msg_show` event)\r\n- The `msg_show` event handler emits a message with a different kind\r\n- We return to the original `\"lua_print\"` batch loop, the assertion finds that `msg_ext_kind` has changed (raising an abort)\r\n\r\n[empty string is emitted]: https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/521eddec7c243a8166609bf1734cb940e9510fc5/src/nvim/message.c#L2357-L2365",
    "closed_at": null,
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "@bfredl you know the messages code very well, can I get your opinion? This approach I've taken doesn't feel quite right (`msg_ext_skip_flush` feels like it belongs just to `msg_ext_ui_flush`)\r\n\r\nShould `msg_puts_len()` be stashing and restoring `msg_ext_kind` instead of dropping the message? (like [`verbose_enter`](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/521eddec7c243a8166609bf1734cb940e9510fc5/src/nvim/message.c#L3549-L3560))",
